<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net" modified="2026-08-03T13:00:00.000Z" agent="datashapes" version="24.0.0">

  <diagram name="1 · Data at every step" id="shapes">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1400" dy="900" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1400" pageHeight="1500" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" /><mxCell id="1" parent="0" />

        <mxCell id="t" value="What the data looks like at every step" style="text;html=1;fontSize=22;fontStyle=1;align=left;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="20" width="800" height="30" as="geometry" /></mxCell>
        <mxCell id="t2" value="Following one real ingredient — &quot;1101 ( ii )&quot; from a Parle-G biscuit label — the whole way through." style="text;html=1;fontSize=13;fontColor=#555555;align=left;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="50" width="1000" height="20" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="d0" value="STEP 0 — INPUT&#10;&#10;Parle-Gmain.jpeg  ·  about 200 KB of image data&#10;&#10;No schema. Just bytes. Not resized, not cropped." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#F5F5F5;strokeColor=#999999;fontSize=12;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="95" width="1320" height="80" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="d1" value="STEP 1 — ExtractedItem     what is PRINTED&#10;&#10;item_id           10               a number so later steps can point at this item&#10;position          7                where it appears; items from one bracket share a position&#10;nesting_depth     0                0 = top level, 1 = inside a bracket, 2 = inside that&#10;parent_item_id    null             what it sits inside, if anything&#10;verbatim          &quot;1101 ( ii )&quot;    exactly as printed — note the spaces inside the brackets&#10;name_as_declared  null             null, because only a code was printed&#10;declared_role     &quot;FLOUR TREATMENT AGENT&quot;     the functional class that introduced it&#10;role_source       &quot;group_heading&quot;  how it was attached: a bracket, not a colon&#10;declared_code     &quot;1101 ( ii )&quot;    the code as printed, untouched&#10;code_system       &quot;bare&quot;           E, INS, bare or none — which system was on the pack&#10;percentage        null&#10;emphasis          false            is it styled differently from the text around it&#10;footnote_marker   null             a + or # tying it to a footnote&#10;confidence        1.0&#10;&#10;THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO &quot;is_additive&quot; FIELD AND NO CANONICAL ID.&#10;The model records what is printed. A later step decides what it means." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#E8DAEF;strokeColor=#7D3C98;fontSize=11;fontFamily=Courier New;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="200" width="640" height="360" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="d1b" value="AND THE CONTAINER AROUND THOSE ITEMS&#10;&#10;declaration_verbatim   &quot;INGREDIENTS : REFINED WHEAT FLOUR (MAIDA)...&quot;&#10;language               &quot;en&quot;&#10;items                  [ ...12 items... ]&#10;unparsed_fragments     []          text the parser FAILED on&#10;warnings               [ ... ]&#10;allergen_statements    [&quot;CONTAINS : WHEAT, MILK&quot;]&#10;footnotes              {&quot;#&quot;: &quot;(D - GLUCOSE, LEVULOSE )&quot;}&#10;declaration_statements [&quot;CONTAINS ADDED FLAVOUR (...)&quot;]&#10;&#10;&#10;WHY FOUR SEPARATE BUCKETS&#10;&#10;Each was added because text was disappearing.&#10;&#10;A label says &quot;CONTAINS PERMITTED NATURAL COLOUR(S)&quot; — that is not an&#10;ingredient, and it is not a parse failure. It is a regulatory statement,&#10;and it means there is a colour in the product that is never named.&#10;For EU export that is a problem. It needed its own home or it was lost.&#10;&#10;unparsed_fragments means THE PARSER FAILED. Everything else has a place." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FDFEFE;strokeColor=#7D3C98;fontSize=11;fontFamily=Courier New;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="720" y="200" width="640" height="360" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="d2" value="STEP 2 — ResolvedItem     what it IS&#10;&#10;item_id                   10               ← THE ONLY LINK BACK. Nothing is copied.&#10;canonical_ins             &quot;1101(ii)&quot;       normalised — the spaces are gone&#10;eu_canonical_id           null             not in EU additive law&#10;classification            &quot;enzyme&quot;         a DIFFERENT regulation governs it&#10;normalised_role           &quot;Flour treatment agent&quot;&#10;codex_functional_classes  [&quot;Flavour enhancer&quot;]&#10;resolution_method         &quot;code_exact&quot;     HOW it was found&#10;resolution_confidence     0.98             comes from the method, never from a model&#10;flags                     [&quot;role_class_mismatch&quot;]&#10;candidates                []&#10;matched_on                &quot;1101(ii)&quot;&#10;&#10;role_class_mismatch:  the label says FLOUR TREATMENT AGENT.&#10;The international standard says papain is a FLAVOUR ENHANCER.&#10;We record the disagreement. We do not resolve it.&#10;&#10;THIS STEP READS ONLY 5 OF THE 14 FIELDS ABOVE:&#10;item_id, declared_code, name_as_declared, declared_role, verbatim" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#D1F2EB;strokeColor=#148F77;fontSize=11;fontFamily=Courier New;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="585" width="640" height="350" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="d3" value="STEP 3 — category candidates     what KIND OF FOOD&#10;&#10;component_label  null      null means the whole product&#10;query&#10;  text           &quot;REFINED WHEAT FLOUR SUGAR REFINED PALM OIL&#10;                  IODISED SALT MILK SOLIDS&quot;&#10;  fields_used    [&quot;ingredient_names&quot;]&#10;  additive_ids   [&quot;330&quot;, &quot;503&quot;, &quot;500(ii)&quot;, &quot;472e&quot;]&#10;top3&#10;  11.1  Sugars and syrups...        0.66&#10;  7.2   Fine bakery wares           0.65   ← THE CORRECT ANSWER, RANK 2&#10;  11.2  Other sugars and syrups     0.65&#10;&#10;&#10;This is the weak point of the whole system, and it is measured:&#10;&#10;   right answer ranked first        53%&#10;   right answer in the top three    82%&#10;&#10;Which is exactly why the next thing that happens is a person confirming." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#E8DAEF;strokeColor=#7D3C98;fontSize=11;fontFamily=Courier New;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="720" y="585" width="640" height="350" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="d4" value="STEP 4 — ItemVerdict     what the LAW says&#10;&#10;item_id            10&#10;eu_canonical_id    null&#10;additive_name      &quot;Papain&quot;&#10;component_label    null&#10;headline           &quot;out_of_scope&quot;&#10;verdict_certainty  &quot;certain&quot;&#10;category_sensitive false&#10;by_category        []            empty — no lookup was needed&#10;flags              [&quot;enzyme_reg_1332_2008&quot;]&#10;&#10;&#10;For an additive that IS assessed, by_category holds one entry per candidate:&#10;&#10;   fcs_code          &quot;7.2&quot;&#10;   category_name     &quot;Fine bakery wares&quot;&#10;   rank              1&#10;   verdict           &quot;permitted_with_conditions&quot;&#10;   max_level_mg_kg   null&#10;   max_level_basis   &quot;gmp&quot;&#10;   conditions        &quot;Permitted via Group I, Additives; ML = quantum satis;...&quot;&#10;   source_url        &quot;https://ec.europa.eu/food/food-feed-portal/...&quot;&#10;   retrieved_date    &quot;2011-12-01&quot;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#D1F2EB;strokeColor=#148F77;fontSize=11;fontFamily=Courier New;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="960" width="640" height="400" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="funnel" value="THE FUNNEL NARROWS&#10;&#10;   14 fields written by extraction&#10;    →  5 read by the resolver&#10;    →  3 read by the category step&#10;&#10;Each step consumes LESS than the last.&#10;That is why the boundaries hold, and why&#10;adding a field upstream cannot break&#10;anything downstream.&#10;&#10;&#10;THE WORKED EXAMPLE, END TO END&#10;&#10;label prints        &quot;FLOUR TREATMENT AGENT [1101 ( ii )]&quot;&#10;extraction records  declared_code &quot;1101 ( ii )&quot;, code_system &quot;bare&quot;&#10;resolver normalises &quot;1101(ii)&quot;, finds PAPAIN in the standard&#10;                    label and standard disagree on function → flagged&#10;                    absent from EU additive law → &quot;enzyme&quot;&#10;rule engine         out_of_scope — a different regulation governs enzymes&#10;&#10;&#10;WHY THIS ONE CASE MATTERS&#10;&#10;A naive check would reason: &quot;not in the list, therefore not permitted&quot;&#10;— and fail a perfectly legal biscuit." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FCF3CF;strokeColor=#B7950B;fontSize=12;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="720" y="960" width="640" height="400" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="l1" value="item_id" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;html=1;strokeWidth=2;fontSize=11;" edge="1" parent="1" source="d1" target="d2"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" /></mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l2" value="item_id" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;html=1;strokeWidth=2;fontSize=11;" edge="1" parent="1" source="d2" target="d4"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>

  <diagram name="2 · Every experiment" id="experiments">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1400" dy="900" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1400" pageHeight="1300" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" /><mxCell id="1" parent="0" />

        <mxCell id="t" value="Every experiment, and why" style="text;html=1;fontSize=22;fontStyle=1;align=left;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="20" width="800" height="30" as="geometry" /></mxCell>
        <mxCell id="t2" value="All scored against hand-checked answers. All logged, each row tagged with which version of the answer key it was scored against." style="text;html=1;fontSize=13;fontColor=#555555;align=left;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="50" width="1100" height="20" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="e1" value="SEARCH METHODS — four of them&#10;&#10;                                    first   top3&#10;   embeddings, plain                 0.41   0.65&#10;   embeddings + component name       0.53   0.82   ← ADOPTED&#10;   keyword search (TF-IDF)             —    0.24   rejected&#10;   keyword + component name            —    0.41   rejected&#10;   a vector database (ChromaDB)      0.41   0.65   identical, not adopted&#10;   vector db + component name        0.53   0.82   identical&#10;   diversifying results (MMR 0.7)    0.53   0.82   no change at all&#10;   diversifying results (MMR 0.5)    0.53   0.82   no change at all&#10;   combining dense + keyword         0.53   0.76   WORSE&#10;   rephrasing the query (MQR)          —      —    ran out of quota&#10;&#10;&#10;WHAT WE EXPECTED AND GOT WRONG&#10;&#10;We predicted keyword search might win on such a small corpus.&#10;It scored less than half. The prediction was wrong, which is&#10;exactly why the control was worth running." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FDFEFE;strokeColor=#7D3C98;fontSize=12;fontFamily=Courier New;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="95" width="650" height="330" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="e2" value="WAYS OF WRITING THE CATEGORY DESCRIPTIONS — seven of them&#10;&#10;                                    first   top3&#10;   full text + component name        0.53   0.82   ← THE ORIGINAL WINS&#10;   strip the boilerplate             0.47   0.55&#10;   full text, no component name      0.41   0.53&#10;   name only + component name        0.41   0.71&#10;   name only                         0.35   0.71&#10;   name plus examples                0.35   0.59&#10;   name plus permitted additives       —    ≈baseline&#10;&#10;&#10;THE NULL NOBODY HAD TESTED&#10;&#10;Every earlier experiment ADDED or REMOVED text around a description.&#10;Nobody had tried the category NAME ALONE.&#10;&#10;The names already read like food descriptions:&#10;   15.1  Potato- cereal- flour- or starch-based snacks&#10;   3     edible ices&#10;&#10;So it was plausible the legal prose was diluting the signal.&#10;&#10;IT WAS NOT. The descriptions carry real signal. Refuted." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FDFEFE;strokeColor=#7D3C98;fontSize=12;fontFamily=Courier New;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="730" y="95" width="650" height="390" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="e3" value="WHAT WENT INTO THE QUERY&#10;&#10;   + the component's own name       +0.17 on BOTH search methods&#10;                                     the single biggest lever found&#10;&#10;   + a product description,&#10;     on every query                  no net gain — it FIXED the product&#10;                                     queries and BROKE the component ones&#10;                                     (telling the retriever a seasoning&#10;                                      was a potato chip)&#10;&#10;   + a product description,&#10;     product queries only            best MRR, lower top-3&#10;&#10;&#10;A RESULT THAT SURPRISED US&#10;&#10;Adding &quot;Glucose biscuits&quot; to the Parle-G query pushed the CORRECT&#10;category OUT of the top three.&#10;&#10;The same change pushed the correct category INTO first place for&#10;the noodles label.&#10;&#10;Same intervention. Opposite effects." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FCF3CF;strokeColor=#B7950B;fontSize=12;fontFamily=Courier New;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="455" width="650" height="380" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="e4" value="THINGS WE BUILT AND THEN REMOVED&#10;&#10;CROPPING THE PHOTO&#10;   Theory: small print needs more pixels, so crop to the panel and enlarge.&#10;   Measured across 10 labels: identical results where it worked, and on&#10;   one label it cropped the wrong part and returned ZERO items instead of 25.&#10;   → turned off by default&#10;&#10;ASKING THE MODEL HOW CONFIDENT IT WAS&#10;   It said 0.0 while confidently identifying a food label.&#10;   Every item came back at exactly 1.0.&#10;   → replaced with a count of evidence found&#10;&#10;A VECTOR DATABASE&#10;   Produced byte-identical results to plain search, at extra setup cost.&#10;   → implemented, measured, not adopted&#10;&#10;A SECOND FRAMEWORK (LangChain)&#10;   Would have rewritten three tested call sites for identical behaviour,&#10;   losing caching and error handling built from real failures.&#10;   → not adopted&#10;&#10;MICROSERVICES&#10;   One user, one process, all Python. None of the reasons to split applies.&#10;   → not adopted" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FADBD8;strokeColor=#943126;fontSize=12;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="730" y="515" width="650" height="400" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="e5" value="THE CONCLUSION FROM ALL OF IT&#10;&#10;Twenty-plus configurations. Four search methods. Seven corpus constructions.&#10;Two ways of combining rankings. Nothing beat where we started.&#10;&#10;And the residual failures do not look like search failures. Look at what the&#10;system has to work with for a biscuit:&#10;&#10;      &quot;REFINED WHEAT FLOUR, SUGAR, REFINED PALM OIL&quot;&#10;&#10;That describes a biscuit. It also describes a cake, a cracker, and a pastry.&#10;Equally well.&#10;&#10;The information needed to tell them apart IS NOT ON THE LABEL.&#10;No search method recovers information that is not there.&#10;&#10;So we stopped tuning, wrote down what we had measured, and built the&#10;confirmation step instead. Category accuracy stopped being something to&#10;fix and became a property of the task." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FDFEFE;strokeColor=#333333;fontSize=13;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;strokeWidth=2;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="870" width="1340" height="300" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>

  <diagram name="3 · Scores and limits" id="scores">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1400" dy="900" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1400" pageHeight="1100" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" /><mxCell id="1" parent="0" />

        <mxCell id="t" value="How it is measured, and what we are not claiming" style="text;html=1;fontSize=22;fontStyle=1;align=left;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="20" width="900" height="30" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="m" value="THE MECHANISM&#10;&#10;Two folders with the same filenames:&#10;&#10;   data/golden/    the RIGHT answer, written by hand, never regenerated&#10;   data/outputs/   what the system produced this time&#10;&#10;A script compares them and prints a score.&#10;&#10;IF THE SCORE DROPS, THE SCRIPT EXITS WITH AN ERROR.&#10;&#10;So a change that breaks something fails loudly instead of silently.&#10;That is what let us try 20+ configurations without losing track." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FDFEFE;strokeColor=#666666;fontSize=12;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="80" width="650" height="240" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="s" value="THE SCORES&#10;&#10;READING THE LABEL&#10;   57 of 57 additive codes found                        1.00&#10;&#10;IDENTIFYING WHAT THEY ARE&#10;   202 items — number, EU code, classification    1.00 / 1.00 / 1.00&#10;&#10;FINDING THE FOOD CATEGORY&#10;   right first    0.53        right in top three    0.82&#10;   The weak point, and the reason a person confirms.&#10;&#10;THE ASSISTANT&#10;   22 items:  15 correct  ·  0 WRONG  ·  4 correctly declined&#10;&#10;THE WHOLE PIPELINE END TO END&#10;   Not measured. It would need expert-verified categories,&#10;   which we do not have. Stated as a limit, not estimated." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#D1F2EB;strokeColor=#148F77;fontSize=12;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="730" y="80" width="650" height="310" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="a" value="WHY THE ASSISTANT'S SCORES ARE NEVER COMBINED&#10;&#10;   correct              15&#10;   wrong                 0     ← the number that matters&#10;   correctly declined    4&#10;   wrongly declined      3&#10;&#10;A WRONG suggestion is dangerous, because a person may accept it&#10;without checking. A DECLINE only leaves work undone.&#10;&#10;Combining them into one &quot;86% accuracy&quot; figure would hide exactly the&#10;distinction that matters.&#10;&#10;AND DECLINING IS A SUCCESS, NOT A FAILURE&#10;&#10;All four genuinely ambiguous items were declined rather than guessed:&#10;   modified cornstarch  ×2   17 possible matches&#10;   calcium phosphate          3 possible matches&#10;   Stevia                     4 possible matches&#10;&#10;The label genuinely does not say which. Declining is correct." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FCF3CF;strokeColor=#B7950B;fontSize=12;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="350" width="650" height="340" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="ns" value="WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT SCORED&#10;&#10;Free-text fields are excluded from every score:&#10;   declared_role, verbatim, model confidence&#10;&#10;They vary between runs. We watched &quot;Emulsifiers&quot; become &quot;Emulsifiors&quot;&#10;on the same image, on the same model.&#10;&#10;NO VERDICT DEPENDS ON THEM, SO NO METRIC SHOULD.&#10;&#10;Scoring something the system does not depend on would make the metric&#10;worse, not stricter — you would get failures that mean nothing." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FDFEFE;strokeColor=#148F77;fontSize=12;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="730" y="420" width="650" height="240" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

        <mxCell id="lim" value="WHAT WE ARE NOT CLAIMING&#10;&#10;THE TEST SET IS 12 LABELS.  That is small. Any difference under about 0.10 on the category metrics is&#10;one or two items and should be treated as noise.&#10;&#10;THE CORRECT FOOD CATEGORIES WERE AI-DRAFTED AND NOT EXPERT-VERIFIED.  So those numbers are useful for&#10;comparing configurations against each other. They are not a validated accuracy claim.&#10;&#10;ALL THE LABELS ARE INDIAN.  The multilingual handling built for European packs was never exercised.&#10;&#10;END-TO-END ACCURACY IS UNMEASURED.  Extraction at 1.00 and identification at 1.00 do not imply the&#10;verdict is right, because category retrieval sits between them at 0.53 and determines the outcome.&#10;&#10;THE SYSTEM READS WHAT A LABEL DECLARES.  It cannot detect undeclared additives, adulterants or&#10;contamination. Border alerts on Indian food exports have largely involved unauthorised dyes —&#10;substances illegal in both countries and therefore never printed on a pack. That needs laboratory&#10;analysis, not label reading.&#10;&#10;CONDITIONS TEXT IS SHOWN, NOT INTERPRETED.  A permission reading &quot;only tuna&quot; narrows below its&#10;category, and the system does not evaluate that.&#10;&#10;CARRY-OVER IS NOT MODELLED.  An additive may be lawfully present in a compound food if it was&#10;permitted in one of that food's ingredients. The engine does not know this.&#10;&#10;ONLY TOP-LEVEL COMPONENTS ARE SCOPED.  A biscuit nested inside a centre inside a chocolate bar is&#10;attributed to the centre, not given its own category." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FADBD8;strokeColor=#943126;fontSize=12;align=left;spacingLeft=12;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=10;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="720" width="1340" height="330" as="geometry" /></mxCell>

      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>

</mxfile>
